{"name":"Bloodstained","hex":"#980001","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bloodstained","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Stained, spotted or otherwise discolored with blood.","Having the color of something which has been stained with blood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bloodstained"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blood residue","blocks":[{"text":"Blood residue are the wet and dry remnants of blood, as well the discoloration of surfaces on which blood has been shed. In forensic science, blood residue can help investigators identify weapons, reconstruct a criminal action, and link suspects to the crime. In archaeology, it can be used to detect of origin of blood stains on buried objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Policarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author%27s_bloodstain.jpg/330px-Policarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author%27s_bloodstain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_residue","wikidata":"Q4927896"},{"title":"Bloodstain pattern analysis","blocks":[{"text":"Bloodstain pattern analysis (BPA) is a forensic discipline focused on analyzing bloodstains left at known, or suspected crime scenes through visual pattern recognition and physics-based assessments. This is done with the purpose of drawing inferences about the nature, timing and other details of the crime. At its core, BPA revolves around recognizing and categorizing bloodstain patterns, a task essential for reconstructing events in crimes or accidents, verifying statements made during investigations, resolving uncertainties about involvement in a crime, identifying areas with a high likelihood of offender movement for prioritized DNA sampling, and discerning between homicides, suicides, and accidents. Not to be confused with haemotaphonomy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Examples_of_Blood-Spatter_and_Droplet_patterns.svg/330px-Examples_of_Blood-Spatter_and_Droplet_patterns.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloodstain_pattern_analysis","wikidata":"Q2478662"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Policarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author%27s_bloodstain.jpg/500px-Policarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author%27s_bloodstain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Policarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author%27s_bloodstain.jpg/500px-Policarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author%27s_bloodstain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" SEM micrograph of a human bloodstain. Pseudo-colored image using software coupled with the microscope, in order to improve the realistic appearance of the stain. The bar corresponds to 10 micrometers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolicarp_Hortol%C3%A0._Coloured_SEM_image_of_an_author's_bloodstain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Hortol%C3%A0_hc%2Bjc_EA_6_Copyright_Taylor_%26_Francis.png/500px-Hortol%C3%A0_hc%2Bjc_EA_6_Copyright_Taylor_%26_Francis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Hortol%C3%A0_hc%2Bjc_EA_6_Copyright_Taylor_%26_Francis.png/500px-Hortol%C3%A0_hc%2Bjc_EA_6_Copyright_Taylor_%26_Francis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"SEM micrographs of a collared peccary experimental bloodstain on chert, aged for one year. Top: a hecatocyte, an erythrocyte with a moon-like shape where plasma, upon drying, contracts and delineates the cell; bottom: a janocyte (n), a negative replica of an erythrocyte, where t